  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nLatest surface analysis places a broad ~1030 mb surface high \nfrom the northern Great Lakes eastward into Quebec. The western \nperiphery of the high is progged to strengthen across the \nnorthern Lakes today, in association with strong subsidence and \nbuilding mid-level heights in the wake of a positive-tilt short \nwave digging southeast across the northeastern CONUS. Farther to\nthe southwest, a slow- moving closed upper-level low was \nevident in GOES vapor imagery over eastern IA. This feature is \nforecast to continue to drift slowly east-southeast across \nnorthern IL through this evening and eventually into central IN \nWednesday morning, forming a Rex block (upper level high \npoleward of an upper level low) which will only gradually shift \neast through mid-week.\n\nCurrent region radar imagery depicts showers (with even some \nisolated lightning strikes) associated with the aforementioned \ncirculation over eastern IA and vicinity. These showers will \ngradually drift east with the circulation today, although \nthey'll be encountering gradually drier air with eastward \nextent. Would expect at least some spotty precipitation to \nspread into the forecast area however, and have added some \nslight chance pops (15-20% - highest west) developing from west \nto east today/early this evening though coverage may be isolated\nin many areas under otherwise mostly cloudy skies. Spotty, \nlight shower chances should diminish from west to east across \nnorthern this evening as the mid-level circulation continues to \ndrift east, and across northwest Indiana overnight, though \nprobably can't completely rule out a few stray sprinkles from \nthe western fringes of this disturbance even into Wednesday \nmorning. As previous day shift noted, there is a bit of a signal\nin guidance for slightly better low-level lapse rates over Lake\nMichigan on Wednesday which could support some lake-effect \nsprinkles or light showers into northeast IL, though delta-Ts \nare marginal (10-12C) and inversion heights are near/just below \n5 kft. Thus chances continue to appear fairly low and kept only \nsub-15% pops without a precip mention near the lake for this \npossibility.\n\nOtherwise, with the relatively strong surface high off to our \nnortheast, breezy/blustery (gusting 20-25 kts at times, \nespecially near the lake) northeast winds will continue across \nthe area into Wednesday before diminishing more substantially. \nThis will maintain dangerous swim conditions (and current Beach \nHazard Statements in effect) for our Lake Michigan beaches, as \nwell as cooler than normal temperatures through mid-week. \nDaytime highs are expected to remain in the 60s with overnight \nlows in the 50s in most spots, though some late night 40s are \npossible away from the lake where breaks in the clouds or more \nwidespread scattering occurs. \n\nEventually, the Rex block is progged to break down in the \nThursday and Friday time period, allowing upper-level ridging to\nextend into the upper Mississippi Valley/western Lakes. This \nwill allow for a modest warming trend into the low 70s and upper\n60s, lighter winds and possibly more sunshine. Medium-range \nguidance continues to depict increasing precip chances west of \nthe Mississippi by Friday however, which may at least spread \nmid/high-level cloud cover back across the region. Current \nglobal models/ensembles suggest precip may try to push into the \nforecast area by late Saturday/Sunday, though with guidance \ndeveloping a deep trough/closed low to our east along the \nAtlantic coast, the eastward progression of any substantial \nprecipitation threat may be somewhat blocked until beyond the \nend of the current forecast period.\n\nRatzer",
